  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Contents]/fn[node_content]` is valid. It is written as fenced commentary on an explicit function with a body, and it gives a substantive reason for the exception. The function is a deliberate panic guard. The test consumer is meant to exercise only storage delegation, and the `Contents` trait forces it to implement this callback. Calling the callback would break the test's invariant that storage never reads graph contents. The guard is meant to stay unexecuted.\n\nThe explanation agrees with the current source. The `Storage` impl for `StoredGraph` (`load_evidence` and `store_evidence`) delegates to `FileEvidenceStorage` using only `self.node_path(node)`. It never calls `node_content`. The panic message in the function body matches the stated purpose. The commentary also says other fixtures exercise content resolution. That is a plausible claim and the exception does not depend on it. The citation is a `cite` role, which is what the built-in permits, and the item has no `impl` or `test` references to make it redundant.",
